          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Antonín Dvořák</strong><br>\nAntonín Dvořák was born in Nelahozeves, Bohemia, which is now part of the Czech Republic. From a young age, he was drawn to music and began his training with the local schoolmaster. His early musical influences were German and Austrian composers, which shaped his initial compositions. His talent was evident as he mastered violin and piano, laying the foundation for his future career.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Dvořák's Musical Education</strong><br>\nDvořák attended the Prague Organ School, where he received a rigorous education in musical theory and composition. Despite struggling with financial difficulties, he completed his studies with distinction. It was during this time that he began to compose his first major works, showing a unique blend of classical traditions with his own personal style.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Rise to Fame</strong><br>\nDvořák's work caught the attention of Johannes Brahms, who became an advocate for his music. With Brahms' help, Dvořák published several of his compositions, which gained widespread popularity. His Moravian Duets and Slavonic Dances were particularly well-received for their use of traditional Czech musical themes.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
