          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Dvořák's American Influence</strong><br>\nIn 1892, Dvořák moved to the United States to direct the National Conservatory of Music in New York City. Here, he discovered American music, which influenced his compositions. The 'New World Symphony,' his most famous piece, was inspired by the Native American music and African-American spirituals he encountered in America.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Compositions in America</strong><br>\nDuring his time in the United States, Dvořák wrote several other significant works. Besides the 'New World Symphony,' he composed the 'American' String Quartet and the 'Cello Concerto in B minor,' which became cornerstones in their respective genres, admired for their incorporation of American elements into classical music.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Return to Europe</strong><br>\nDvořák returned to his homeland in 1895. He continued to compose, conduct, and support young musicians. His later years were marked by the creation of symphonic poems and operas. He became the director of the Prague Conservatory, shaping the future of Czech music with his progressive musical philosophies.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
