          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Dvořák's Legacy</strong><br>\nDvořák's contributions to music extended beyond his compositions. He influenced many composers with his use of folk music and helped establish a sense of national identity within Czech music. Today, he is remembered as one of the great figures of Czech nationalism in music.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Personal Life and Character</strong><br>\nDvořák was known for his humble beginnings and remained modest throughout his life despite his fame. He was a family man devoted to his wife and children. His character was reflected in his music, which was often infused with a sense of joy and reverence for the natural world.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Death and Posthumous Recognition</strong><br>\nDvořák died in 1904 in Prague from heart failure. After his death, his music continued to be performed and celebrated. Over time, he has been recognized as a major figure in the development of classical music and remains one of the most respected composers of the Romantic era. His work continues to influence musicians and composers around the world.</p>\n"
              },
